From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 02:57:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] app ids: chat.delta -> chat.slothful

Every reverse-DNS identifier the app owns moves to the slothful.chat
namespace, so the fork installs next to Delta Chat instead of replacing
it, and the two never talk to each other's processes:

- bundle IDs of the app and all extensions/targets (chat.slothful,
  .DcShare, .DcNotificationService, .DcWidget, .Clip, .DcTests,
  .DcCore) and the app clip's parent-application identifier
- app group + shared keychain group (group.chat.slothful.ios) in all
  entitlements and in the Swift constants that open the shared
  container, UserDefaults suite and keychain
- the chat.slothful.deeplink URL scheme (widget, share extension, app
  clip -> app); with both apps installed the old scheme would route to
  whichever registered last
- Darwin notification names between app and notification service;
  these are system-wide, so the old names would cross-talk with an
  installed Delta Chat
- CFBundleURLName labels, os_log subsystem, a dispatch queue label

Not changed: DEVELOPMENT_TEAM / KeychainManager.teamId still name
upstream's Apple team; device builds need our own team ID there.
Simulator builds and tests do not sign, so they are unaffected.
Associated domains (applinks:i.delta.chat) are left as is; they can't
verify for our team, so universal links just won't open this app.

DcTests/AppIdentifierTests checks the bundle ID and the deeplink
scheme of the running host app.
